REGIONAL REGULATION OF PALEMBANG CITY

NUMBER 9 OF 2022

CONCERNING

PROTECTION OF WOMEN AND CHILDREN FROM ACTS OF VIOLENCE

BY THE GRACE OF ALMIGHTY GOD

MAYOR OF PALEMBANG,

Considering:

a. that every citizen has the right to obtain protection and guarantee of their human rights, and has the right to receive fair treatment to obtain equal opportunities and benefits in achieving life welfare;
b. that laws and regulations governing the protection of women and children in regulating efforts for women and children to be free from acts of violence in the region require regulations that can guarantee their implementation;
c. that all forms of acts of violence against citizens, especially women and children, constitute a form of violation of human rights, therefore the state, government, society, and family have the obligation and responsibility for the protection of women and children from acts of violence;
d. that based on the considerations as referred to in letter a, letter b, and letter c, it is necessary to establish a Regional Regulation on the Protection of Women and Children from Acts of Violence;

In View of:

1. Article 18 paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;
2. Law Number 28 of 1959 concerning the Establishment of Level II Regions Including Municipalities, Within the Level I Region of South Sumatra (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1959 Number 73, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 1821);
3. Law Number 39 of 1999 concerning Human Rights (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1999 number 165, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3886);
4. Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2002 Number 109, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4235) as amended by Law Number 35 of 2014 concerning Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 297, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5606);
5. Law Number 23 of 2004 concerning the Elimination of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2004 Number 95, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4419);
6. Law Number 13 of 2006 concerning Protection of Witnesses and Victims (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2006 Number 64, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4635) as amended by Law Number 31 of 2014 concerning Amendment to Law Number 13 of 2006 concerning Protection of Witnesses and Victims (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 293, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5602);
7. Law Number 21 of 2007 concerning the Eradication of the Criminal Act of Trafficking in Persons (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2007 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4720);
8. Law Number 25 of 2009 concerning Public Services (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2009 Number 112, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5038);
9. Law Number 11 of 2012 concerning the Juvenile Criminal Justice System (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2012 Number 153, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5332);
10. Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 244, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5587) as amended several times, most recently by Law Number 1 of 2022 concerning Financial Relations between the Central Government and Regional Governments (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2022 Number 4, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia 6757);
11. Government Regulation Number 4 of 2006 concerning the Implementation and Cooperation for the Recovery of Victims of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2006 Number 15, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4604);
12. Government Regulation Number 9 of 2008 concerning Procedures and Mechanisms for Integrated Services for Witnesses and/or Victims of the Criminal Act of Trafficking in Persons (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2008 Number 22, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4818);
13. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment Number 2 of 2008 concerning Guidelines for the Implementation of Women's Protection;
14. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment Number 3 of 2008 concerning Guidelines for the Implementation of Child Protection; 15. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 19 of 2011 concerning Guidelines for the Empowerment of Women Victims of Violence (State News of the Republic of Indonesia of 2011 Number 903);
16. Regional Regulation Number 16 of 2010 concerning Protection of Women and Children Victims of Violence (Regional Gazette of South Sumatra Province of 2010 Number 3 Series E);
17. Regional Regulation Number 3 of 2017 concerning the Implementation of Family Resilience Development (Regional Gazette of South Sumatra Province of 2017 Number 3);

With Joint Approval

REGIONAL HOUSE OF REPRESENTATIVES OF PALEMBANG CITY

and

MAYOR OF PALEMBANG

DECIDES:

To Establish:

REGIONAL REGULATION CONCERNING THE PROTECTION OF WOMEN AND CHILDREN FROM ACTS OF VIOLENCE.

Article 2

The protection of women and children from acts of violence is implemented based on the principles of:
a. respect for and fulfillment of the rights of victims;
b. justice and gender equality;
c. local wisdom;
d. non-discrimination;
e. the best interests of women and children;
f. active participation; and
g. restoring the situation.

Article 3

The regulation of the protection of women and children from acts of violence aims to:
a. prevent violence against women and children;
b. protect and provide a sense of security for women and children;
c. provide services to women and children victims of acts of violence; and
d. empower women victims of violence.

Article 4

The scope of regulation of the protection of women and children from acts of violence includes:
a. forms of acts of violence;
b. rights and obligations;
c. protection of women and children;
d. guidance and supervision; and
e. funding.

CHAPTER II

FORMS OF ACTS OF VIOLENCE

Article 5

Forms of acts of violence include:
a. physical violence;
b. psychological violence;
c. sexual violence;
d. neglect; and
e. exploitation.

Article 7

Women and children from acts of violence receive the following rights:
a. the right to have their dignity respected as human beings;
b. the right to health and psychological recovery from the suffering experienced by victims;
c. the right to determine their own decisions;
d. the right to obtain information;
e. the right to confidentiality of their identity;
f. the right to restitution;
g. the right to social rehabilitation;
h. the right to complaint handling;
i. the right of children to education;
j. to obtain facilities in the judicial process;
k. the right to assistance.

Article 8

1. In efforts to protect women and children from acts of violence, the City Government is obligated to:
a. guarantee the rights of women and children without discrimination in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations;
b. establish and implement policies, programs and activities for the protection of the rights of women and children;
c. facilitate the formation of community organizations concerned with women and children;
d. cooperate with the community and/or community organizations and the private sector in the implementation of the protection of women and children from acts of violence;
e. carry out rehabilitation and empowerment of women and children who are victims of acts of violence;
f. provide special protection to women and children victims of various acts of violence; and
g. take preventive action in order to prevent violations of the rights of women and children.
2. The responsibility in efforts to protect women and children from acts of violence as referred to in paragraph (1) is carried out by regional apparatus in accordance with their duties and functions.
3. Further provisions regarding the obligations of the City Government as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be regulated by Mayor Regulation.

Article 11

1. In order to prevent violations of the rights of women and children, the City Government:
a. conducts socialization regarding the prevention of violence against women and children by optimizing the role of community units, community leaders, religious leaders, community organizations and mass media;
b. provides guidelines on handling acts of violence against women and children;
c. develops an integrated database and information system on acts of violence against women and children;
d. strengthens the function of community organizations engaged in the protection and advocacy of women and children; and
e. provides public facilities that are comfortable and safe for women and children.
2. The responsibility of the City Government in efforts to protect women and children from acts of violence as referred to in paragraph (1) is carried out by regional apparatus for women and children in accordance with their duties and functions.
3. Further provisions regarding planning for the prevention of violations of the rights of women and children are integrated by the City Government into regional development planning.

Article 14

1. The implementation of handling women and children victims of acts of violence includes:
a. handling complaints of victims of violence;
b. health services; and
c. law enforcement and legal assistance.
2. The implementation of handling women and children victims of acts of violence as referred to in paragraph (1) is carried out through integrated services.
3. Integrated services as referred to in paragraph (2) for women and children victims of acts of violence are carried out with the principles of:
a. responsive;
b. non-discrimination;
c. equal and respectful relationships;
d. maintaining confidentiality;
e. providing a sense of security and comfort;
f. respecting individual differences;
g. non-judgmental;
h. respecting the choices and decisions of victims;
i. sensitive to the background and condition of victims;
j. using language that is appropriate and understood by victims;
k. fast and simple;
l. empathy;
m. fulfillment of the rights of women and children; and
n. legal certainty.

Article 15

Handling of women and children victims of acts of violence as referred to in Article 14 paragraph (1) letter a is carried out by:
a. hospitals;
b. community health centers;
c. Technical Implementation Unit for the Protection of Women and Children;
d. Safe houses;
e. Women Crisis Centre;
f. Marriage Advisory, Guidance and Preservation Agency;
g. Police;
h. Prosecutor's Office;
i. Courts;
j. Ministry of Law and Human Rights;
k. Legal Aid Institutions; and
l. Other Institutions.

Article 21

1. Social rehabilitation services as referred to in Article 20 paragraph (2) letter a aim to:
a. assist victims in recognizing their problems and finding effective ways to overcome them independently;
b. empower victims to determine their future;
c. provide moral support for victims in facing the legal process they are undergoing; and
d. make victims feel accepted in the community environment.
2. Counseling services are conducted by gender-responsive social rehabilitation officers, consisting of:
a. counselors who have obtained certificates;
b. psychologists; and
c. psychiatrists.
3. Counseling services are conducted in a place that guarantees a sense of security, comfort and confidentiality of information from victims.
4. The stages of counseling services include:
a. initial counseling;
b. follow-up counseling;
c. case discussion; and
d. site visits.
